===============================
🧩 PART 1 — TABULAR REGRESSION
===============================
🎯 Problem Being Solved

We want to predict a numeric target (e.g., spend, score, value) from structured customer features.

This is a regression problem.

🧠 Why a Feedforward Neural Network (FFNN)?

Tabular data:

Has no spatial structure (like images)

No sequence (like text)

Each row is an independent observation

👉 Best baseline = Fully Connected Network (FFNN)

Why NOT CNN / RNN here?
Model	Reason Not Used
CNN	No spatial locality
RNN	No sequence dependency
Transformer	Overkill
🧱 Architecture Used
Input → Dense → ReLU → Dense → ReLU → Dense → Output

Why ReLU?

Prevents vanishing gradients

Fast computation

Standard choice for hidden layers

Why Linear Output?

Regression needs unbounded continuous output

🔧 Why These Preprocessing Steps?
1. Missing Value Handling

Neural networks:

❌ Cannot handle NaNs

❌ Will break gradient computation

So we:

Median for numeric (robust)

Mode for categorical (preserves meaning)

2. One-Hot Encoding

Neural networks only understand numbers, not categories.

3. Feature Scaling (VERY IMPORTANT)

Why scaling is mandatory:

NN uses gradient descent

Large feature values dominate gradients

Leads to unstable training

So we used StandardScaler.

4. Clipping
np.clip(X, -10, 10)


Why?

Prevent exploding gradients

Improve numerical stability

👉 This is advanced DL hygiene.

📉 Loss Function: MSE

Why MSE?

Penalizes large errors more

Smooth and differentiable

Standard for regression

🎯 Outcome of Part 1

Model learns complex non-linear relationships

Training & validation losses converge

RMSE evaluates real-world error

===============================
🧩 PART 2 — NLP TEXT CLASSIFICATION
===============================
🎯 Problem Being Solved

We want to classify text (reviews, complaints, feedback).

Text is:

Variable length

Discrete (words)

Order matters

🧠 Why We Can’t Use FFNN Directly

Text ≠ numbers.

So first, we must:

Convert text → numbers

Preserve meaning

🔹 Step 1: Tokenization
"I love this product"
→ [12, 57, 4, 89]


Why?

Neural networks require numeric input

🔹 Step 2: Padding

Why padding?

Batches need same length

Enables vectorized computation

🔹 Step 3: Embedding Layer (CRITICAL)
What Embedding Does

Instead of one-hot vectors:

Learns dense semantic representations

Example:

"good", "great", "excellent" → nearby vectors


This allows:

Semantic learning

Context capture

🧠 Models Used
🔹 Baseline: Embedding + Pooling

Why?

Simple

Fast

Strong baseline

How it works:

Embed words

Average meaning across sentence

Classify

🔹 RNN (LSTM)

Why RNN?

Text has order

Meaning depends on sequence

Why LSTM (not SimpleRNN)?

Handles long dependencies

Solves vanishing gradient problem

📉 Loss Function

Binary Cross-Entropy:

Probabilistic output

Stable gradients

Standard for binary classification

🎯 Outcome of Part 2

High accuracy

Correct classification

Demonstrates:

Representation learning

Sequential modeling

===============================
🧩 PART 3 — COMPUTER VISION
===============================
🎯 Problem Being Solved

Classify images into categories.

Images have:

Spatial structure

Local patterns

Translation invariance

🧠 Why CNN (Convolutional Neural Network)?

CNNs exploit:

Locality

Shared weights

Hierarchical features

👉 FFNN would explode in parameters.

🧱 CNN Architecture Used
Conv → ReLU → Pool → Conv → ReLU → Pool → Dense → Output

Why Convolution?

Detect edges, textures, shapes

Same filter across image

Fewer parameters

Why MaxPooling?

Downsamples

Adds translation invariance

Reduces overfitting

Why Flatten + Dense?

Convert spatial features to decision space

📉 Loss Function

Sparse Categorical Cross-Entropy:

Multiclass classification

Efficient label encoding

🔍 Evaluation

Accuracy

Confusion matrix

Misclassified image analysis

This proves:

The model generalizes, not memorizes.

